4. [''cap.''] In ''American hist.'', a member of the British party during the Revolutionary period; a loyalist. {{smaller|The Tories were very numerous, especially in the Middle and Southern colonies, and many of them took arms for the king. They were frequently severely persecuted, and after the war many of them emigrated to Canada and elsewhere.}}

{{smaller block|Washington will not trust us with the keeping of a suspected ''Tory'', if we let the rascal trifle in this manner with the corps.{{right|''J. F. Cooper'', The Spy, xxix.}}}}

5. [''cap.''] In general, a conservative; one who favors established authority and institutions, especially in a monarchy or an aristocracy; a person of aristocratic principles, as opposed to a democrat or a radical.

{{smaller block|Purrus Ram and Khoom Dass are in attendance, and fear greatly that the party of the Viziers, to whom they are opposed, will hurl them from power, and that the ''Tories'' of Bussahir will triumph.{{right|''W. H. Russell'', Diary in India, II. 191.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''High Tory''', an upholder or advocate of an extreme type of Toryism.}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Pertaining to or characteristic of tories, in any sense; specifically [''cap.''], belonging or relating to the Tories: as, a ''Tory'' government; ''Tory'' principles or measures. See I.

{{smaller block|"Surrender! you servants of King George," shouted the leader, . . . "or I will let a little of your ''tory'' blood from your veins."{{right|''J. F. Cooper'', The Spy, xxii.}}}}

{{smaller block|The party led by Sir Robert Peel no longer called itself "''Tory'','' but "Conservative." ''Contemporary Rev.'', LI. 4.}}

{{smaller block|'''Tory Democracy''', the principles or views of the Tory Democrats; also, the Tory Democrats collectively.—'''Tory Democrats''', in ''recent British politics'', those members of}}

readings.c1s2:
the Conservative party who are supposed to incline more or less to democratic ideas and methods.

¶
'''toryism''' (tō′ri-izm), ''n.'' [< ''tory'' + ''-ism.''] The principles, methods, and practices of tories, in any sense; specifically [''cap.''], those of the British Tories.

{{smaller block|Nothing would illustrate the subject better . . . than an inquiry into the rise and progress of our late parties, or a short history of ''toryism'' and whiggism from their cradle to their grave, with an introductory account of their genealogy and descent. {{right|''Bolingbroke'', Parties, ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|The times have been dreadful, and old families like to keep their old tenants. But I dare say that is ''Toryism.'' {{right|''George Eliot'', Felix Holt, viii.}}}}

'''Toryminæ''' (tor-i-mī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Torymus'' + ''-inæ.''] A notable subfamily of parasitic hymenopterous insects, of the family ''Chalcididæ'', conspicuous from their brilliant metallic colors and their long ovipositor: originally named as a family ''Torymidæ'' by Watson in 1833. {{smaller|They are the commonest parasites of the cynipid and cecidomyidan gall-makers, although some have been reared from the cells of burrowing bees and a few from lepidopterous larvæ. About 200 species are known.}}

'''Torymus''' (tor′i-mus), ''n.'' [NL. (Dalman, 1820).] A genus of hymenopterous parasites of gall-making insects, typical of the subfamily ''Toryminæ.''

'''tory-rory†''' (tō′ri-rō′ri), ''a.'' [Appar. a varied redupl. of ''tory.''] Wild; boisterous; harum-scarum.

{{smaller block|Lift up your voices, and sing like nightingales, you ''tory rory'' jades. Courage, I say; as long as the merry pence hold out, you shall none of you die in Shoreditch. {{right|''Dryden'', Kind Keeper, iv. 1.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
Specifically—2. To tease (wool). ''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 497.

[Obsolete or prov. Eng. in both uses.]

'''toser''' (tō′zėr), ''n.'' [Also ''tozer''; < ''tose'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who toses; specifically, a teaser of wool. ''Pop. Sci. Mo.'', XXXV. 812. [Prov. Eng.]

'''tosh'''<sup>1</sup> (tosh), ''a.'' [Said to be < OF. ''tousé'', ''touzé'', clipped, shorn, pared round, < L. ''tondere'', pp. ''tonsus'', clip, shear: see ''tonsure''.] Neat; trim. [Scotch.]

{{smaller block|The hedges will do; I clipped them wi' my ain hand last back-end; and, nae doubt, they make the avenue look a hantle ''tosher''. {{right|''Wilson'', Margaret Lindsay, p. 271.}}}}

'''tosh'''<sup>2</sup> (tosh), ''n.'' A variant of ''tush''<sup>1</sup>. ''Halliwell.''

'''toshach''', ''n.'' See ''toisech''.

'''toshake†''', ''v. t.'' [ME. ''toshaken''; < AS. ''tōsceacan'', shake to pieces, < ''tō-'', apart, + ''sceacan'', shake: see ''to-''<sup>2</sup> and ''shake''.] To shake violently; shake to pieces.

{{smaller block|Glad was he to londe for to hye,<br>So was he with the tempest al ''toshake''. {{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 962.}}}}

'''toshear†''', ''v. t.'' [ME. ''tosheren''; < AS. ''tōsceran'', cut apart, < ''tō-'', apart, + ''sceran'', shear: see ''to-''<sup>2</sup> and ''shear''<sup>1</sup>.] To cut in two.

{{smaller block|The God of love . . . al ''toshare''<br>Myn herte with his arwis kene. {{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 1858.}}}}

'''toshend†''', ''v. t.'' [ME. ''toshenden''; < ''to-''<sup>2</sup> + ''shend''.] To ruin utterly; destroy.

{{smaller block|I had been deed and al ''toshent''<br>But for the precious oynement. {{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 1903.}}}}

