{{smaller block|beauty.{{right|''Goldsmith'', The Bee, No. 2.}}}}

'''To toss in a blanket''', to toss (a person) upward from a blanket held slackly at the corners and edges, and jerked vigorously up and down, the person tossed being sometimes thrown as high as the ceiling. This was formerly a favorite form of the expression of popular dislike. It is also practised in schools, among sailors, etc. Compare ''haze''<sup>2</sup>, ''v. t.'', 2, ''hazing.''

{{smaller block|A rascally slave! I will ''toss'' the rogue ''in a blanket.''{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., ii. 4. 240.}}}}

{{smaller block|I shall certainly give my solitary voice in favour of religious liberty, and shall probably be ''tossed in a blanket'' for my pains.{{right|''Sydney Smith'', To Lady Holland, Jan. 17, 1813.}}}}

'''To toss off.''' (''a'') To take off; drink off, as a dram.

{{smaller block|For in a brave vein they ''tost off'' the bouls.{{right|''Robin Hood and Maid Marian'' (Child's Ballads, V. 375).}}}}

{{smaller block|The corporal produced the bottle and the glass, poured it out, made his military salute, and ''tossed'' it ''off.''{{right|''Marryat'', Snarleyyow, xxxii.}}}}

(''b'') To dispose of; pass off; while away: said of time.

{{smaller block|Have you read Cynthia? It is a delightful thing to ''toss off'' a dull hour with.{{right|''S. Judd'', Margaret, ii. 1.}}}}

'''To toss the oars''' (''naut.''). See ''oar''<sup>1</sup>.—'''To toss up''', to prepare hastily, especially by cooking.

{{smaller block|On Saturday stew'd beef, with something nice,<br />Provided quick, and ''toss'd up'' in a trice.{{right|''W. King'', The Vestry.}}}}

{{smaller block|Amid these rich and potent devices of the culinary art . . . poor Hepzibah was seeking for some nimble little titbit, which, with what skill she had, and such materials as were at hand, she might ''toss up'' for breakfast.{{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, vii.}}}}
